          "opencode-route/haiku-plan#low",
          "opencode-route/beam-plan"
        ],
        "selection": "round-robin",
        "autonomy": "rules"
      },
      "background": {
        "name": "Background - patient queue work: best value first, no latency contract",
        // NEW 2026-10-27 (pool redesign): the tier for machinery-dispatched
        // work no human watches — repo sweeps, issue-queue workers,
        // stewardship cycles, batch classification. Ordered chain in value
        // order: DeepSeek Flash at #high first (workhorse-grade when it is
        // available), then the MiMo pool (the fleet's cheapest burn) for
        // the bulk.
        // 2026-10-28: the clock-gated chain designed under
        // opencode#639 is LIVE (named windows landed in fork PR #649;
        // gates are the target `window` field — a window name, or
        // { name, invert: true } for the inverse). DeepSeek deductions
        // double in the peak windows — Mon-Fri 01:00-04:00 +
        // 06:00-10:00 UTC — on Go, and ClinePass's DeepSeek reference
        // rates split the same way (verified against DeepSeek's own
        // pricing page 2026-10-29; the one clause not encoded is the
        // Chinese-public-holiday exclusion — see the
        // model_route_windows comment below and opencode#821), so
        // during peak this chain leads
        // with MiMo and keeps Flash as the fallback, and off-peak it
        // leads with Flash. The shipped Window schema holds ONE
        // daily range per named window, so the two peak ranges are
        // two named windows (deepseek-peak-early / deepseek-peak-late
        // in experimental.model_route_windows below) and the chain
        // composes them into exactly three effective stages:
        //   1. deepseek-flash-plan#high, all doors, OFF-PEAK
        //   2. mimo-flash-plan
        //   3. deepseek-flash-plan#high, all doors, PEAK
        // Positions: the two MiMo positions gated ON each peak
        // window lead during their window; the Flash position gated
        // on the INVERSE of deepseek-peak-early leads in every other
        // regime (all off-peak hours, and as the late-peak fallback
        // behind the late MiMo position); the Flash position gated
        // ON deepseek-peak-early is the early-peak fallback; the
        // final ungated MiMo position is the off-peak failover.
        // No agent defaults here yet — queue workers are repointed as the
        // follow-up step so the adoption is observable.
        "targets": [
          {
            "model": "opencode-route/mimo-flash-plan",
            "window": "deepseek-peak-early"
          },
          {
            "model": "opencode-route/mimo-flash-plan",
            "window": "deepseek-peak-late"
          },
          {
            "model": "opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan#high",
            "window": { "name": "deepseek-peak-early", "invert": true }
          },
          {
            "model": "opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan#high",
            "window": "deepseek-peak-early"
          },
          "opencode-route/mimo-flash-plan"
        ],
        "selection": "ordered",
        "autonomy": "rules"
      },
      "workhorse-ordered": {
        "name": "Workhorse ordered - DeepSeek Flash plan first, strictly ordered",
        // 2026-11-03 (Drew): a dedicated ordered pool in FRONT of
        // workhorse-primary inside the workhorse chain, seating ONLY
        // deepseek-flash-plan#high. Workhorse traffic tries this pool
        // first; the pool yields nothing ready when every Flash door is
        // walled or demoted, and the chain falls through to
        // workhorse-primary unchanged. deepseek-flash-plan KEEPS its
        // workhorse-primary seat (weight 2) in the same edit - this is
        // an addition, not a move.
        "targets": ["opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan#high"],
        "selection": "ordered",
        "autonomy": "rules"
      },
      "workhorse-primary": {
        "name": "Workhorse primary - Codex (Luna) + Meta (Spark contributor) + DeepSeek Flash plan + Sonnet plan (Antigravity)",
        // 2026-10-31 (Drew): primary swaps Terra -> Luna #xhigh to start (high also approved). AA v5 cost-per-score: Luna beats Terra at EVERY effort level (cost per 100 score pts - low 0.021 vs 0.241, medium 0.067 vs 0.447, high 0.091 vs 0.619, xhigh 0.114 vs 0.889, max 0.184 vs 1.882 USD); both burn the same ChatGPT pair, so the efficiency is pure quota yield. Terra kept its specialist-tier seats at that writing (corrected 2026-11-03, task 8486 / issue #4390: Terra #medium was removed from specialist on 2026-10-27 and left the workhorse relief pool on 2026-10-31, so it now holds no specialist or workhorse seat), where its higher score ceiling (51 max vs Luna 38) earns its cost.
        // 2026-10-31 (Drew): primary swaps to Codex — terra-plan#medium takes the primary seat to burn the Codex pool down (Drew resets the quota once it is used up); Gemini Flash moves to workhorse-weighted at weight 2.
        // 2026-10-31 (Drew): Sonnet leaves primary — primary is Gemini Flash alone. Sonnet moves to workhorse-weighted.
        // 2026-10-31 (Drew): primary is Antigravity-only. The DeepSeek
        // Flash and Muse Spark contributor legs are removed from
        // workhorse-primary; DeepSeek keeps its own routes and Spark
        // keeps its workhorse-weighted seat — only the primary changed.
        // 2026-10-30: the Spark leg ran through muse-proxy
        // (provider "muse", muse/muse-spark-1.3) — the direct meta
        // door is dead (attestation; see the "muse" provider note).
        // 2026-10-30 (amendment, Drew): the leg now seats
        // muse-direct/muse-spark-1.3-contributor (Responses direct
        // mode replaced both older Meta doors). Same weight 1, same
        // round-robin position.
        // TEMPORARY (2026-10-29, Drew, code#2173): workhorse's primary
        // slot round-robins between the DeepSeek plan route
        // (deepseek-flash#high — the standard operating procedure's
        // primary, unchanged) and the direct Meta door
        // meta/muse-spark-1.3 — that exact model on the Muse Spark
        // plan (Meta Muse Code High Usage), deliberately NOT the
        // contributor pool: the point is to wear down that specific
        // plan's quota, which was pacing to strand ~half its weekly
        // allowance at the 2026-11-02 reset while DeepSeek burned
        // alone. Equal weights = ordinary round-robin alternation.
        // Spark ALSO keeps its existing later seat in this tier
        // (workhorse-weighted -> spark-plan#xhigh); this wrapper is
        // additive and changes nothing else.
        // 2026-10-29 (Drew, code#2173): a THIRD leg joins the burn —
        // antigravity-agy/gemini-4-argon#high, Gemini 4 Argon through
        // the Antigravity agy door. The Antigravity GEMINI weekly
        // pool read 5% used against a hard reset Fri 2026-10-31
        // 13:11Z — ~95% of it would strand unused. Probe-verified
        // before this edit (2026-10-29): a live call landed in the
        // route_attempt ledger as provider antigravity-agy, model
        // gemini-4-argon, variant high, outcome success, and every
        // gemini-* model reads the same shared weekly pool in the
        // quota gatherer, so Argon draws that pool. Weights below:
        // Argon takes 2 of 4 picks (~half of primary traffic — the
        // largest share that does not starve the other two burns);
        // DeepSeek and Spark keep 1 each, Spark still burning toward
        // its 2026-11-02 reset; if the Gemini 5-hour window walls,
        // quota demotion sheds overflow back to the other legs by
        // itself. TO UNDO JUST THE GEMINI LEG: delete the argon
        // target and the "weights" map — this wrapper returns to the
        // DeepSeek/Spark two-way shape above.
        // TO REVERT to the all-DeepSeek standard operating procedure:
        // delete (or comment out) this route and point workhorse's
        // first target back at "opencode-route/deepseek-flash#high".
        // 2026-10-30 (route audit): the Meta leg's model changed from
        // meta/muse-spark-1.3 to meta/muse-spark-1.3-contributor. Same
        // direct meta door, same single Meta plan, same weight — the
        // plain SKU now fails 100% from this client: route_attempt
        // shows 8/8 HTTP 403 since 2026-10-30 03:40Z, "This API key is
        // billed to a Muse Code subscription. Muse Code subscriptions
        // cover requests from the Muse Code client only." (the active
        // meta credential is the Muse Code plan key), while the
        // contributor SKU on the same key keeps succeeding (286
        // successes on 2026-10-29). The leg still burns the Meta plan
        // toward the 2026-11-02 reset as intended; revert this one
        // line if Meta serves the plain SKU to this client again.
        // 2026-10-30 (Drew): Sonnet joins the temporary primary
        // round-robin — opencode-route/sonnet-plan#high at weight 1.
        // Its live door is antigravity-ls/claude-sonnet-5-5, and the
        // Antigravity Claude pool sits nearly full and idle, so this
        // also puts that pool to work. The Argon leg below has been
        // configured since 2026-10-29 but the router has yet to send
        // it route traffic (direct sessions work) — that defect lives
        // in the opencode router, not in this config.
        // 2026-10-30 (Drew): two leg changes. (1) The Spark leg moves
        // to the contributor SKU (seated as
        // muse-direct/muse-spark-1.3-contributor after the amendment
        // above) --
        // probe-verified through the muse program 2026-10-30 (usage
        // recorded against the contributor id), and muse-proxy's
        // resolver passes the contributor id upstream verbatim since
        // PR #2762. (2) The Gemini leg switches from Argon to Flash:
        // Argon's first token lands at 7-9s against the router's 10s
        // first-token failover budget (6 of 23 attempts timed out and
        // failed over), so the leg mostly produced stalls; Drew
        // declined raising the budget. The leg is now
        // opencode-route/gemini-flash-plan#high
        // (antigravity-ls/gemini-3.8-flash -- the tool-capable Gemini
        // door) at the same weight 2. Argon stays seated in
        // argon-plan; the 2026-10-29 Argon note above is history.
        // 2026-11-02 (Drew, direct order): Meta joins as CO-PRIMARY
        // alongside Codex. muse-direct/muse-spark-1.3-contributor
        // takes a primary seat at weight 2, equal with luna-plan's
        // 2 — round-robin co-primaries. Why now: the Meta pool reset
        // 2026-11-02T00:00:00Z fresh at 0% (100 pts, required
        // ~14.9/day to land at reset) while Codex projects
        // exhaustion tonight; muse-direct was re-enabled this
        // morning after its 2026-11-01 park. Spark ALSO keeps its
        // workhorse-weighted seat (spark-plan#xhigh) — this seat is
        // additive, the same shape as the 2026-10-29 wrapper.
        // 2026-11-02 (effort audit, Drew asked): the Spark leg takes its
        // #xhigh pin, matching every other Spark seat in the fleet
        // (workhorse-weighted relief, specialist, speed) and the
        // price-per-point rulings behind them: AA/workbook Spark
        // xhigh II 45 @ $1.37/task = $0.030/pt beats max II 48 @
        // $1.60 = $0.033/pt, and Drew dropped specialist Spark
        // max -> xhigh 2026-11-01 on the same math. The Luna leg
        // was already correct: luna-plan#xhigh at $0.114 per 100
        // score pts (AA v5) is the Luna elbow - max jumps to $0.184
        // for its 38-pt ceiling. The bare Spark seat was the only
        // unpinned effort in the tier (ledger: variant NULL).
        // 2026-11-02 (Drew, refinement): the Luna leg seats the
        // openai-plan leaf directly (openai-plan/gpt-6-luna#xhigh)
        // instead of routing through luna-plan: the workhorse-only
        // rule - Luna only from the OpenAI plans, the doors with no
        // DeepSeek - belongs to this seat, not to the shared plan
        // route. luna-plan keeps its full pool (see its own note).
        // 2026-11-02 (Drew): DeepSeek Flash plan moves INTO primary
        // from workhorse-weighted: opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan#high
        // takes a primary seat at weight 2, equal with Luna and Spark -
        // three-way round-robin. Its weighted-pool seat is removed in
        // the same edit (a move, not an addition); workhorse-weighted
        // keeps haiku-plan#high w2 and spark-plan#xhigh w1. Side
        // benefit: a third of primary picks leave the Meta pool, which
        // is pacing to exhaust days before its Nov 9 reset; quota
        // demotion sheds any walled Flash door on its own.
        // 2026-11-03 (Drew): sonnet-plan#medium joins primary. This seats
        // the Antigravity Claude/GPT bucket (0% used at the order, resets
        // Nov 9 09:00Z) in the workhorse rotation — it is the Claude/GPT
        // bucket, NOT the Gemini bucket pulled from the primary path on
        // 2026-10-31. Medium effort: workhorse is the volume tier;
        // sonnet-plan#high stays specialist's head seat.
        "targets": [
          "openai-plan/gpt-6-luna#xhigh",
          "muse-direct/muse-spark-1.3-contributor#xhigh",
          "opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan#high",
          "opencode-route/sonnet-plan#medium"
        ],
        "selection": "round-robin",
        "autonomy": "rules",
        "weights": {
          "openai-plan/gpt-6-luna": 2,
          "muse-direct/muse-spark-1.3-contributor": 2,
          "opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan": 2,
          "opencode-route/sonnet-plan": 2
        }
      },
      "workhorse": {
        "name": "Workhorse - standard work: Flash primary, weighted relief pool behind it",
        // 2026-10-27 pool redesign (Drew's structure): incremental —
        // DeepSeek Flash #high is the primary every session tries first;
        // the weighted relief pool (workhorse-weighted) absorbs failover
        // and overflow. This replaces the flat weighted round-robin of
        // the 2026-10-26 balance pass, whose seats were re-litigated in
        // the redesign review:
        // - Luna #xhigh OUT: dominated — wherever Flash is available it
        //   covers Luna's niche, and the seat only survived by inertia.
        // - Sol #low OUT: workhorse keeps exactly ONE OpenAI seat
        //   (Terra #medium — the ladder's efficiency winner, II 41.3 at
        //   $0.095/task with a 4.90s TTFT (AA v5.0; the 1.43s figure
        //   previously cited here was the AA v4.3.2-era reading); Sol's low rung was a second
        //   seat on the same meter at the same job.
        // - Spark contributor #xhigh stays, at weight 1 in the relief
        //   pool: it is the tier's Meta/vendor-diversity seat (its edge
        //   is the separate Meta quota).
        // The relief pool's members are unchanged in kind from the
        // balance pass (AA v5): Haiku #high II 30 / 26.7s TTFT; Terra
        // #medium II 38 / 4.90s (AA v5.0; 1.43s was the v4.3.2-era
        // reading); Spark #xhigh / 19.1s / ~300 t/s.
        // 2026-10-29 (code#2173): the primary target below is the
        // TEMPORARY workhorse-primary wrapper (round-robin of
        // deepseek-flash#high, meta/muse-spark-1.3, and
        // antigravity-agy/gemini-4-argon#high) — see that route's
        // comment for the revert steps.
        // 2026-11-03 (Drew): workhorse-ordered (deepseek-flash-plan
        // only, ordered) joins the chain IN FRONT of workhorse-primary.
        // deepseek-flash-plan is NOT removed from workhorse-primary.
        "targets": [
          "opencode-route/workhorse-ordered",
          "opencode-route/workhorse-primary",
          "opencode-route/workhorse-weighted"
        ],
        "selection": "ordered",
        "autonomy": "rules"
      },
      "workhorse-weighted": {
        "name": "Workhorse relief - weighted pool behind the Flash primary",
        // The weighted half of workhorse's incremental structure: sessions
        // that fail over (or are demoted off) the Flash primary draw here
        // by weight. Not addressed by any agent directly.
        // 2026-10-28 (Job 2): the Spark seat addresses spark-plan#xhigh
        // (was the direct meta contributor leaf) at the same weight 1.
        // 2026-10-31 (Drew): Sonnet and DeepSeek Flash plan join the relief pool (Spark already seated).
        // 2026-10-31 (Drew): Terra leaves the relief pool for the primary seat; Gemini Flash joins here at weight 2 (the weight it carried in primary).
        // 2026-10-31 (Drew): Antigravity OUT of the primary path. The
        // Antigravity Gemini bucket was burning ~95 pts/day organically
        // and projecting exhaustion a week before its reset, so both
        // Antigravity-drawing members leave this pool: gemini-flash-plan
        // (single-door antigravity-direct/gemini-3.8-flash — the Gemini
        // bucket) and sonnet-plan (live door antigravity-direct/
        // claude-sonnet-5-5, claude-code leaf dormant — the Claude/GPT
        // bucket). workhorse-primary already seats Terra alone, so the
        // whole workhorse stack now draws zero Antigravity. The plan
        // routes themselves are untouched (switchboard rule): Gemini
        // Flash and Sonnet stay seated in the deliberate tiers
        // (specialist, summit, speed) exactly as before.
        // 2026-11-02 (Drew): DeepSeek Flash plan leaves this pool -
        // it moves up into workhorse-primary at weight 2 (see that
        // route's note). Haiku and Spark keep their seats and weights.
        // 2026-11-03 (Drew): deepseek-flash-plan#high rejoins this
        // pool at weight 2 (it also keeps its workhorse-primary seat
        // and its new workhorse-ordered seat - additions only).
        "targets": [
          "opencode-route/haiku-plan#high",
          "opencode-route/spark-plan#xhigh",
          "opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan#high"
        ],
        "selection": "weighted",
        "autonomy": "rules",
        "weights": {
          "opencode-route/haiku-plan": 2,
          "opencode-route/spark-plan": 1,
          "opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan": 2
        }
      },
      "specialist": {
        "name": "Specialist - hard work: quality first, Claude then Argon then Meta Spark then OpenAI",
        // 2026-10-26 pass 3 (AA figures re-baselined to v5 2026-10-31, #1673): Sonnet #high II 41 / TTFT ~12s /
        // $1.64 per task (AA v5 costing),
        // Spark contributor #max / 42.0s / 176 t/s (single Meta door is the caveat), Sol
        // #medium II 46 / TTFT 3.62s / $0.36 — that family's elbow and this chain's value star, Pro
        // #high last (TTFT ~2s class; fires only when every seat above is walled).
        // Luna #max REMOVED in pass 3: at II 28 (AA v5) it sat under the pool's band with a
        // 93.8s TTFT — the balance rule's remove case; Sol already covers the OpenAI door.
        // 2026-10-26: Terra #medium (AA v5 II 38 @ $0.17/task — its family's
        // elbow and OpenAI's default rung) slots in above Pro: it out-scores Pro's band
        // band at half of Sol's price. Per-rung TTFT is unpublished tonight (only max's
        // 78s is known); acceptable risk in an ordered chain's relief seat, and issue
        // #1249 tracks the missing numbers. Workhorse carries Terra #medium too, at
        // weight 1 until the TTFT lands.
        // 2026-10-27 pool redesign: Argon #high inserts at #2 — Gemini 4
        // Argon (AA high II 53, CAI 64) out-evidences Spark on coding and
        // gives the chain a non-Claude/OpenAI second seat on its own
        // (Go contributor) meter, touching neither the Antigravity quota
        // the Sonnet head depends on nor the OpenAI reserve. Terra
        // #medium REMOVED: behind Sol #medium it was
        // the same effort on the same doors and the same quota — illusory
        // diversity (if Sol walls on quota, Terra walls too). DeepSeek Pro
        // tail REMOVED: its <=36 ceiling is below this tier's band.
        "targets": [
          "opencode-route/sonnet-plan#high",
          "opencode-route/argon-plan#high",
        // 2026-11-01 (Drew): Spark seat drops max -> xhigh. AA v5: max scores 36 vs xhigh 34 — two points for roughly double the reasoning time (max measured 540s/task). Spark in specialist is the Meta-diversity seat behind Sonnet/Argon; xhigh keeps the failover responsive. This was the last #max target in the tier routes.
          "opencode-route/spark-plan#xhigh",
          "opencode-route/sol-plan#medium"
        ],
        "selection": "ordered",
        "autonomy": "rules"
      },
      "summit": {
        "name": "Summit - very complex design only, deliberate use",
        // 2026-10-30: the Wonder leaf ran through muse-proxy
        // (provider "muse", muse/muse-wonder-1.0#max) — the direct
        // meta door is dead (attestation; see the "muse" provider
        // note).
        // 2026-10-30 (amendment, Drew): the leaf now seats
        // muse-direct/muse-wonder-1.0-contributor#xhigh (Responses
        // direct mode replaced both older Meta doors; the #max
        // variant is preserved). Same position in the ordered chain.
        // 2026-10-26: Sol addressed through sol-plan (four doors) instead of a single direct
        // OpenAI leaf; Wonder runs the contributor model (always cheaper on the Meta plan).
        // 2026-10-27 pool redesign: Argon #high inserts at #2 and Sol
        // #xhigh moves LAST. The OpenAI plan is the fleet's deepest
        // reserve, so the chain spends Antigravity (Opus), the Go
        // contributor bucket (Argon — CAI 64, the strongest coding
        // evidence here), and Meta (Wonder) before it touches OpenAI at
        // all; the previous order burned Sol ahead of Wonder, backwards
        // under the reserve rule.
        "targets": [
          "opencode-route/opus-plan#high",
          "opencode-route/argon-plan#high",
          "muse-direct/muse-wonder-1.0-contributor#xhigh",
          "opencode-route/sol-plan#xhigh"
        ],
        "selection": "ordered",
        "autonomy": "rules"
      },
      "speed": {
        "name": "Speed - fastest seats at or above the Haiku bar, for latency-bound work",
        // 2026-10-26 pass 3 (AA figures re-baselined to v5 2026-10-31, #1673): a latency pool is judged on
        // time-to-first-token as much as decode — Spark contributor #xhigh / TTFT 19.1s /
        // 303 t/s, Flash #max II 32 / 1.1s / 170 t/s (plan pool addressed directly, no PAYG
        // tail), Haiku #medium / 11.9s / 174 t/s (the pool's minimum-bar seat, kept for
        // its speed; #xhigh's 67.4s TTFT failed the pool's purpose). Luna REMOVED: its #max
        // paired the pool's slowest decode (~127 t/s) with a 93.8s TTFT.
        // 2026-10-27 pool redesign: one definition of fast — decode
        // throughput at a sensible rung, II floor ~38. Flash drops from
        // #max to #high (max is fast per token but slow per task: the
        // extra output tokens stretch wall-clock), and Haiku rises from
        // #medium (II 34, scout-grade) to #high (II 38 at the same
        // ~190 t/s decode; its 26.7s TTFT is the accepted cost). Beam is
        // faster still (385 t/s) but II 30 is below this pool's floor —
        // it is scout's speed, not this tier's.
        // 2026-10-28 (Job 2): the Spark seat repoints to spark-plan at
        // the same rung and weight, and Gemini Flash joins at weight 1
        // via gemini-flash-plan — antigravity-ls serves
        // gemini-3.8-flash live (AA v5: II 31 at high,
        // ~311 t/s decode), which clears this pool's bar.
        "targets": [
          "opencode-route/spark-plan#xhigh",
          "opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan#high",
          "opencode-route/haiku-plan#high",
          "opencode-route/gemini-flash-plan#high"
        ],
        "selection": "round-robin",
        "autonomy": "rules",
        "weights": {
          "opencode-route/spark-plan": 3,
          "opencode-route/deepseek-flash-plan": 2,
          "opencode-route/haiku-plan": 2,
          "opencode-route/gemini-flash-plan": 1
        }
      },
      "small-100k": {
        "name": "Small 100k - Haiku + Luna below the 100k context cliff",
        // 2026-10-26 (as luna-haiku-100k): both members price much cheaper below 100k
        // context. The route's context cap (developing-today/opencode#554) presents a
        // 100k limit, so sessions pinned here compact before crossing the cliff. On fork
        // builds predating #554 the decoder strips the unknown limit key and the pool
        // simply runs uncapped.
        // AA v5: Haiku #high II 30, Luna #xhigh II 23 (TTFT 24.2s; #max would be 93.8s).
        // 2026-10-27 pool redesign: this is now the <=100k element of the
        // renamed `small` pool (below).
        "targets": [
          "opencode-route/haiku-plan#high",
          "opencode-route/luna-plan#xhigh"
        ],
        "selection": "round-robin",
        "autonomy": "rules",
        "limit": { "context": 100000 }
      },
      "small": {
        "name": "Small - light work in small contexts, Haiku + Luna value seats",
        // RENAMED 2026-10-27 (pool redesign) from luna-haiku-100k. Sessions
        // pinned to the old id need re-pinning. The pool is the fleet's
        // light/small tier: Haiku and Luna are much cheaper in small
        // contexts, so membership is organized by context ceiling —
        //   - the <=100k element (small-100k): Haiku #high + Luna #xhigh
        //   - the <=200k element: Haiku #high alone (Luna's cheap tier
        //     ends at 100k)
        // The route schema has no per-target context limit, so the
        // elements are expressed as a nested route (small-100k, capped at
        // 100k) beside the direct Haiku target, under this route's 200k
        // cap. Haiku 5.5 is 1M native — the 200k element is a cost-policy
        // ceiling, not a model limit: bigger jobs belong to bigger pools.
        "targets": [
          "opencode-route/small-100k",
          "opencode-route/haiku-plan#high"
        ],
        "selection": "round-robin",
        "autonomy": "rules",
        "limit": { "context": 200000 }
      }
    },
    "model_route_windows": {
      // Named route windows (fork PR #649, opencode#639). DeepSeek
      // peak: Mon-Fri 01:00-04:00 and 06:00-10:00 UTC — deductions
      // double on Go in these windows and ClinePass's DeepSeek
      // reference rates split the same way. The shipped Window
      // schema holds one daily range per named window, so the two
      // ranges are two windows; the background route composes them
      // (see its comment for the position-by-position reading).
      // VERIFIED 2026-10-29 against DeepSeek's own pricing page
      // (https://api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing/): "Off-peak
      // rates are half of the peak rates. Peak hours are 01:00 - 04:00
      // and 06:00 - 10:00 UTC, Monday through Friday, excluding Chinese
      // public holidays. All other hours are off-peak, including
      // weekends and Chinese public holidays in full." So the times
      // and the weekend treatment below are exactly right (weekends
      // are off-peak in full — Chinese make-up workdays on a weekend
      // do NOT become peak), but one clause is NOT encoded: a Chinese
      // public holiday that falls Mon-Fri is off-peak for the whole
      // day, and these windows still treat it as peak. The Window
      // schema has no calendar dates, date ranges, or holiday lists
      // and a gate cannot exclude one window from another, so the
      // holiday exclusion cannot be expressed today — tracked in
      // developing-today/opencode#821 (date-aware windows). Until
      // that lands these two windows are the closest correct
      // approximation: on weekday Chinese public holidays the
      // background route leads with MiMo and treats Flash as
      // peak-priced when it is actually off-peak — lost value on
      // those days, never overspend.
      "deepseek-peak-early": {
        "daysOfWeek": ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri"],
        "start": "01:00",
        "end": "04:00",
        "timezone": "UTC"
      },
      "deepseek-peak-late": {
        "daysOfWeek": ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri"],
        "start": "06:00",
        "end": "10:00",
        "timezone": "UTC"
      }
    }
  }
}